# Memento

Capture state so the originator can restore it later **without** leaking internals to the caretaker.

## Anatomy

- Originator: `save()` / `restore(memento)`.
- Memento: immutable copy; caretaker must not poke fields (narrow getter API).
- Caretaker: undo/redo stacks.

```ts
export class CartMemento {
  constructor(private readonly items: CartItem[]) {
    this.items = items.map((item) => ({ ...item }));
  }

  getItems() {
    return this.items.map((item) => ({ ...item }));
  }
}

export class ShoppingCart {
  save() {
    return new CartMemento(this.items);
  }

  restore(memento: CartMemento) {
    this.items = memento.getItems();
  }
}
```

Caretaker as a provider **must not** be a process-wide cart for all users. Use request/session scope, or persist snapshots.

## Nest mapping

- DB transactions are the usual “restore” for failed workflows — not Memento.
- File reorder undo would be persisted versions / event log, not an in-memory stack on `SpacesModule`.
- Pair with Command when undo is a first-class API (`nest-command.mdc`).
- Clone vs snapshot: Prototype copies to create a **new** object; Memento copies to **roll back** the same object.

## When to use

- Undo/redo of an encapsulated in-memory editor.
- Commit/rollback of a unit of work that is not a SQL transaction.

## When not to use

- Trivial state — keep the previous DTO.
- Huge graphs — store deltas.
- Multi-instance API memory stacks.

Related: `nest-command.mdc`, `nest-prototype.mdc`.
